<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xri version="1.0">
   <description>
      <p>astro-pi — unified PixInsight distribution (scarter4work)</p>
      <p>NukeX stacking and stretching module, Planetary video viewer and stacker, and the EZ Stretch BSC scripts. PI Copilot and Gaia Depth Grade are no longer distributed here; installing EZ Stretch 1.0.13 removes their files.</p>
      <p>Repository: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/scarter4work/astro-pi/main/repository/</p>
   </description>
   <platform os="linux" arch="x64" version="1.9.6:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="20261116-linux-x64-NukeX-5.3.1.2.tar.gz" sha1="3b489d0b7648286e998cd813f3acccca146f46fb" type="module" releaseDate="20261116">
         <title>NukeX</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>NukeX 5.3.1.2</b>
            </p>
            <p>Distribution-fitted pixel-signal estimation and auto-stretch for PixInsight. NukeX works on the FITS light frames you add (with optional flats): for every pixel it fits a statistical distribution to every pixel&apos;s samples across the stack, estimates the signal from the fitted model, and applies one of its stretches (Auto is recommended). A camera quantum-efficiency (QE) database built into the module routes each frame and decomposes dual-narrowband colour data (HaO3 / S2O3) into emission lines. Frames open only as local files, and the FITS library has no network or shared-memory drivers. New in 5.3.1.2: Pause/Abort during distribution fitting is seen within a few hundredths of a second. Fitting copies each batch of pixels out of the working record, through the GPU and back, and each copy used to check for Abort only when it had finished: on ten 24-megapixel frames on a busy machine an Abort waited up to 1.2 s to be seen. The copies now check as they go, so the run stops as soon as its working files are released; every stack is unchanged. Also recent: Pause/Abort while frames are measured no longer waits for star detection on frames where a bright nebula fills the field (it took 14-20 s; now about half a second), flats from a colour (OSC) camera are applied to each pixel by its own colour before debayering, the Lupton stretch gives its linear limit at Q = 0 and refuses a stretch value of 0, and CLAHE&apos;s per-channel mode equalizes every channel of a colour image. Built for PixInsight 1.9.6 / PCL 2.13; requires PixInsight 1.9.6 or later. It loads on any Linux with glibc 2.34 or newer (Ubuntu 22.04+, Debian 12+, Rocky/RHEL 9+).</p>
            <p>Network: one kind of connection only, the camera-database update check. It makes HTTPS requests to raw.githubusercontent.com (this repository), automatically at most once every 7 days when you open the NukeX window, and whenever you press Check now; it sends no identifier, usage data or image data. A newer database is downloaded and installed only if you agree, after its Ed25519 signatures are verified. Automatic checks can be turned off in the NukeX window. NukeX launches no external program. Files outside the PixInsight installation: ~/.config/nukex4/ (update-check state, a downloaded database, filter names you teach it, your stretch ratings), a frame cache in ~/.cache/nukex4/cache/ while a run lasts, and, while a run lasts, its progress files in a private directory (mode 0700) under $TMPDIR or /tmp, deleted when the run ends. Documentation, including every network connection, external program and file location: doc/tools/NukeX/NukeX.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="20261110-linux-x64-Planetary-0.5.0.2.tar.gz" sha1="e2464bc6506d47a43a136546d5c38d476eccbfce" type="module" releaseDate="20261115">
         <title>Planetary</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>Planetary 0.5.0.2</b>
            </p>
            <p>Planetary imaging for PixInsight, with two processes. SERViewer opens SER video files and uncompressed 8-bit greyscale or raw Bayer AVI files (such as ASIAIR captures): it shows any frame in a preview, opens it as a PixInsight image, and can save an AVI as a SER file. PlanetaryStack stacks such a video into one image (lucky imaging): it measures every frame, chooses the sharpest frames and aligns them, region by region, with nothing to set, previews the stack with zoom, and opens it as a new image, or says why a capture gives none. The target is read from the ASIAIR file name, and for a colour capture of the Moon not taken through a narrowband or IR-pass filter PlanetaryStack can also make a mineral moon (calibrated, sharpened, with the Moon&apos;s true colour differences amplified), tuned live with Strength, Chroma blur and Sharpen sliders. New in 0.5: for a colour capture of Mercury to Neptune not taken through a narrowband or IR-pass filter, Planet processing makes a finished image in the planet&apos;s natural colour (calibrated to its catalogue reflectance, red and blue aligned against atmospheric dispersion, shown linear and sharpened), tuned live with Saturation, Chroma blur and Sharpen sliders. 0.5.0.2: Third-party notices corrected to PCL 2.13. Built for PixInsight 1.9.6 / PCL 2.13; requires PixInsight 1.9.6 or later.</p>
            <p>Both read only the video file you choose and an AVI&apos;s .txt sidecar. SERViewer writes only the SER file you choose; either process writes, if you click Save pattern..., the .txt sidecar next to the AVI. Neither uses the network or runs external programs. Documentation: doc/tools/SERViewer/SERViewer.html and doc/tools/PlanetaryStack/PlanetaryStack.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
   <platform os="all" arch="noarch" version="1.8.0:2.0.0">
      <package fileName="EZStretch_v1.0.13.zip" sha1="2b34a4575afc127bcc9187ba0d77fe9460f7d540" type="script" releaseDate="20261116">
         <title>EZ Stretch</title>
         <remove>
            src/scripts/scarter4work/EZDonutRepair.js
            src/scripts/scarter4work/EZDonutRepair.xsgn
            bin/PICopilot-pxm.so
            bin/PICopilot-pxm.xsgn
            doc/tools/PICopilot
            src/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade
            doc/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade
            bin/gaia-depth-grade-sidecar
         </remove>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Stretch v1.0.13</b>
            </p>
            <p>Stretches a linear colour image with Lupton RGB, a root-power stretch or PhotometricStretch, with a live preview; the result opens as a new image window. No network connections, no external programs, no files written. Installing this version removes the files of products no longer distributed from this repository, where present: EZ Donut Repair (src/scripts/scarter4work/EZDonutRepair.js and .xsgn), PI Copilot (bin/PICopilot-pxm.so and .xsgn, doc/tools/PICopilot/) and Gaia Depth Grade (src/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade/, doc/scripts/GaiaDepthGrade/, bin/gaia-depth-grade-sidecar). The stretch itself is unchanged. Documentation: doc/scripts/EZStretch/EZStretch.html.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
      <package fileName="EZHazeKill_v1.1.1.zip" sha1="56b20c696453640a7e85da78ee1846b723b17fdf" type="script" releaseDate="20261115">
         <title>EZ Haze Kill</title>
         <description>
            <p>
               <b>EZ Haze Kill v1.1.1</b>
            </p>
            <p>Removes the flat sky pedestal and colour cast that remain after gradient extraction, on linear or stretched images. The sky is measured on the darkest tiles of the image, the background is never raised, and dark pixels roll off smoothly instead of being clipped to zero. Includes its documentation.</p>
            <p>Licence: BSD 3-Clause.</p>
         </description>
      </package>
   </platform>
</xri>
